  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The next morning, Max wakes Nagiyev up at the restaurant. Still groggy, Nagiyev brags that he spent the whole night talking about love with Vika. Listen to the full conversation in Russian first, and then we will walk through every line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дмитрий Владимирович, пожалуйста, просыпайтесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Извините, просто ресторан уже открывается.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы что, опять здесь ночевали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну какое твоё дело?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Что она в тебе нашла?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ведь я настоящий принц.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы о чём вообще говорите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "О нашей Виктории Сергеевне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А она здесь при чём?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Я не выспался всю ночь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы с ней о любви говорили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Понимаешь теперь меня?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Дмитрий Владимирович, пожалуйста, просыпайтесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dmitry Vladimirovich, please wake up.' The grammar packs 'please' and 'wake up' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Извините, просто ресторан уже открывается.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse me, it is just that the restaurant is opening already.' Notice how Russian expresses 'excuse me' and 'opens' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы что, опять здесь ночевали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you spend the night here again?' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'spent the night' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну какое твоё дело?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well what business is it of yours?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'business'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Что она в тебе нашла?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did she find in you?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'what' before landing on 'found'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ведь я настоящий принц.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'After all, I am a real prince.' The meaning hinges on 'after all' working together with 'prince' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы о чём вообще говорите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you talking about at all?' Notice how Russian expresses 'and' and 'speak' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "О нашей Виктории Сергеевне.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'About our Victoria Sergeyevna.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'about' and finishing on 'Sergeyevna'. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А она здесь при чём?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what does she have to do with this?' Max anchors the line on 'and', while 'what' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Я не выспался всю ночь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did not get enough sleep all night.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'got enough sleep' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы с ней о любви говорили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She and I talked about love.' Notice how Russian expresses 'we' and 'spoke' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Понимаешь теперь меня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you understand me now?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to understand' before landing on 'me'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
